/* data/learn-c/phy-12-10.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-10.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-10"] = {"10.1":{"s":"Electric currents and moving charges produce magnetic fields, and a magnetic field pushes on moving charges and on currents. This chapter covers both sides: the force on a charge or a wire in a field, the torque on a current loop (the idea behind motors and galvanometers), and how to find the field of a wire, an arc, a loop, a solenoid and a toroid with the Biot-Savart law and Ampère's law."},"10.2":{"s":"A charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B feels a force F = q(v × B). The force is perpendicular to both v and B, so it changes the direction of motion but never the speed: a magnetic force does no work. It is largest when v is at right angles to B and zero when v is along B. The SI unit of B is the tesla.","f":[{"x":"F = q*v*B*sin(theta)","n":"Size of the magnetic force; theta = angle between v and B. Direction from v × B (reverse it for a negative charge).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.2, p.231"}],"w":["A charge at rest, or moving along B, feels no magnetic force.","For an electron, find v × B and then reverse the direction."]},"10.3":{"s":"When a charge moves at right angles to a uniform B, the magnetic force is always perpendicular to its velocity, so it moves in a circle at constant speed. The radius grows with momentum, but the time for one turn does not depend on the speed. A cyclotron uses this: two hollow D-shaped electrodes with an alternating voltage at this same frequency give the particle a push every half turn, so it spirals outwards gaining energy.","f":[{"x":"R = m*v/(q*B)","n":"Radius of the circle; m*v = momentum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.232"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*m/(q*B)","n":"Time for one full circle; the same for slow and fast particles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.233"},{"x":"f_c = q*B/(2*pi*m)","n":"Cyclotron frequency; the alternating voltage must have this frequency (resonance).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.234"},{"x":"KE = q^2*B^2*R^2/(2*m)","n":"Largest kinetic energy a cyclotron of radius R can give.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.234"}],"w":["The period and frequency do not depend on v or R; only on q, m and B.","The magnetic force does not speed the particle up; the electric field in the gap between the D's does."]},"10.4":{"s":"If the velocity has a part along B as well as a part across it, the two parts behave separately. The part across B makes the charge go round in a circle; the part along B feels no force and stays constant. Together the charge moves on a helix (a spiral) around the field lines, with the same time per turn as in plain circular motion.","w":["Only the velocity part perpendicular to B sets the radius; use v sin θ, not v."]},"10.5":{"s":"A current is a stream of moving charges, so a wire carrying current in a magnetic field feels a force: the sum of the forces on its drifting electrons. For a straight wire the force is perpendicular to both the wire and B. For a wire of any shape, add up (integrate) the forces on its small pieces I dl × B.","f":[{"x":"F = I*L*B*sin(theta)","n":"Force on a straight wire of length L carrying current I; theta = angle between the wire and B. Vector form F = I L × B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.5, p.235"}],"w":["A wire lying along the field feels no force (θ = 0).","Take the direction of L as the direction of the current, not of electron flow."]},"10.6":{"s":"For a closed loop of wire in a uniform magnetic field, the small length vectors dl add up to zero around the loop. So the total magnetic force on any closed current loop in a uniform field is zero. The loop is not pushed as a whole, but it can still feel a torque that turns it.","f":[{"x":"F = 0","n":"Net force on any closed current loop in a uniform field.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.6, p.236"}],"w":["Zero net force does not mean zero torque; see the next topic."]},"10.7":{"s":"In a uniform field two sides of a rectangular loop feel equal, opposite forces along different lines: a couple that turns the loop. The torque is largest when the loop's plane is parallel to B and zero when the plane is perpendicular to B. A motor uses this torque. In a moving coil galvanometer a radial field keeps the torque NIAB, a spring balances it, so the deflection is proportional to the current.","f":[{"x":"tau = N*I*A*B*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a flat coil of N turns, area A; theta = angle between the coil's normal and B. Works for any coil shape.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.7, p.237"},{"x":"phi = (N*A*B/K)*I","n":"Moving coil galvanometer: deflection; K = torsional constant of the spring.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.7, p.238"}],"w":["θ is measured from the NORMAL to the coil, not from the coil's plane.","The radial field keeps sin(θ) = 1 at every deflection, which is why the scale is linear."]},"10.8":{"s":"A current loop acts like a small magnet, so we give it a magnetic dipole moment μ = NIA. Its direction is along the normal to the loop, chosen by the right hand rule from the current's sense. With μ the torque on the loop in a uniform field is written simply as μ × B, just like the torque p × E on an electric dipole.","f":[{"x":"mu = N*I*A","n":"Magnetic dipole moment of a coil; unit A m² (= J/T).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.8, p.238"},{"x":"tau = mu*B*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on the dipole; theta = angle between mu and B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.8, p.238"}],"w":["Convert the area to m² and the current to A before finding μ."]},"10.9":{"s":"A magnetic dipole in a field has potential energy that depends on its angle to the field, just as an electric dipole does in an electric field. The energy is lowest (most stable) when the dipole moment points along B and highest when it points against B.","f":[{"x":"U = -mu*B*cos(theta)","n":"Potential energy of a magnetic dipole; theta = angle between mu and B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.9, p.239"}],"w":["Work needed to turn the dipole from θ₁ to θ₂ = U₂ − U₁ = μB(cos θ₁ − cos θ₂)."]},"10.10":{"s":"The Biot-Savart law gives the field made by a small piece of current-carrying wire. The field falls as the inverse square of the distance, grows with the current and the piece's length, and points along dl × r, so it is perpendicular to both. Adding up all the pieces gives the field of a whole wire. For a very long straight wire the field circles the wire and falls as 1/R.","f":[{"x":"dB = (mu_0/(4*pi))*I*dl*sin(theta)/r^2","n":"Field of a current element I dl at distance r; theta = angle between dl and r.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"},{"x":"mu_0 = 4*pi*10^-7 T m/A","n":"Permeability of free space.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"},{"x":"B = mu_0*I/(2*pi*R)","n":"Field at distance R from a very long straight wire.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"},{"x":"B = mu_0*I/(4*pi*R)","n":"Field at perpendicular distance R from the end of a semi-infinite wire (half of the above).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"}],"w":["Points on the line of the wire itself get no field from it (sin θ = 0).","Direction: thumb along the current, fingers curl the way B goes."]},"10.11":{"s":"Each of two long parallel wires sits in the field of the other, so each feels a force. Currents in the same direction attract; currents in opposite directions repel. The forces on the two wires are equal and opposite. The ampere was defined from this force: 1 A in each of two long wires 1 m apart in vacuum gives a force of 2 × 10⁻⁷ N per metre.","f":[{"x":"F/L = mu_0*I_1*I_2/(2*pi*d)","n":"Force per unit length between two long parallel wires d apart.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.11, p.241"}],"w":["Same direction attract, opposite repel: the reverse of the rule for like charges."]},"10.12":{"s":"For a circular arc of radius r, every small piece of wire is at the same distance from the centre and at right angles to the line joining it, so all the pieces add their fields in the same direction. The field at the centre is proportional to the angle the arc subtends. A full circle is the special case with an angle of 2π.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*I*theta/(4*pi*r)","n":"Field at the centre of an arc that subtends angle theta (in radians).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.12, p.242"},{"x":"B = mu_0*I/(2*r)","n":"Field at the centre of a full circular loop.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.12, p.242"}],"w":["Put θ in radians: a semicircle has θ = π.","Straight wire parts that point at the centre add no field there."]},"10.13":{"s":"On the axis of a circular loop the parts of the field across the axis cancel in pairs, so only the part along the axis is left. The field is strongest at the centre and gets weaker as you move away along the axis.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*I*R^2/(2*(z^2 + R^2)^(3/2))","n":"Field at distance z from the centre along the axis of a loop of radius R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.13, p.243"}],"w":["Put z = 0 to get the centre value μ₀I/(2R)."]},"10.14":{"s":"The field lines of a current loop look like those of a short bar magnet: one face of the loop acts as a north pole and the other as a south pole. The right hand rule fixes which is which. Far away along the axis, the loop's field has the same form as an electric dipole's field, with the magnetic moment m = IA in place of p.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*N*I/(2*R)","n":"Field at the centre of a flat coil of N turns.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.14, p.244"},{"x":"B = mu_0*m/(2*pi*z^3)","n":"Field far away on the axis (z much larger than R); m = I*A = magnetic moment.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.14, p.244"}],"w":["Far field falls as 1/z³, like a dipole, not as 1/z²."]},"10.15":{"s":"Ampère's law: go round any closed loop, add up the part of B along the loop times each small length, and the total equals μ₀ times the net current passing through the loop. Currents outside the loop add nothing to this total. Like Gauss' law for charges, it gives the field quickly when the current is very symmetric, for example a long straight wire.","f":[{"x":"B*2*pi*r = mu_0*I","n":"Ampère's law on a circle of radius r around a long straight wire; gives B = μ₀I/(2πr).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.15, p.246"}],"w":["Give each current a sign with the right hand rule along the loop's direction.","Ampère's law is always true, but it is only easy to use when B is the same all along a well-chosen loop."]},"10.16":{"s":"A solenoid is a long, closely wound coil. Inside it the field is nearly uniform and along its axis; outside it is very weak. Ampère's law on a rectangular loop gives B from the turns per unit length and the current. A toroid is a solenoid bent into a ring: its field lines are circles inside the ring, and the field outside it is zero.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*n*i","n":"Field inside a long solenoid; n = turns per unit length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.16, p.247"},{"x":"B = mu_0*N*i/(2*pi*R)","n":"Field inside a toroid of N turns along the circle of radius R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.16, p.247"}],"w":["n is turns per METRE, not the total number of turns.","The solenoid formula is good in the middle; near the ends the field is about half."]}};
